1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,530
Hill sides explode. Massive blocks of stone break off of solid rock walls.

2
00:00:11,531 --> 00:00:13,400
And powerful

3
00:00:13,401 --> 00:00:22,720
machines crush large rocks in a matter of seconds. Now, quarries on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,280
These solid granite walls are hundreds of feet thick and seemingly impenetrable.

5
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:45,900
But they don't protect a high-level security building for the government or an underground testing site.

6
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:51,560
These walls belong to Rock of Ages Quarry located in Barrie, Vermont.

7
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:56,160
The company is the largest granite quarrier in North America.

8
00:00:56,161 --> 00:00:57,039
Its haul?

9
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:04,560
Over 118,000 tons of rock a year, atop one of the richest deposits of granite

10
00:01:04,561 --> 00:01:05,840
ever discovered.

11
00:01:05,841 --> 00:01:11,600
And this quarry boasts some of the most powerful equipment in the world.

12
00:01:11,601 --> 00:01:16,400
Its drills cut through solid rock at over three feet per minute.

13
00:01:16,401 --> 00:01:22,840
Once free of the wall, huge trucks shuttle the 200-ton granite blocks to towering derricks,

14
00:01:22,841 --> 00:01:27,040
Trains that hoist them cautiously to the surface, 60 stories up.

15
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:35,780
Quarrymen toil doggedly year-round in brutal, ever-changing weather conditions, chipping away at the rock face.

16
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:41,760
This mammoth upheaval of granite stretches for miles across the landscape.

17
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:46,600
Like an iceberg, only its tip is exposed.

18
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:50,800
Its mass extends more than 10 miles below the surface.

19
00:01:52,840 --> 00:02:00,840
This granite deposit, like every other type of rock, was formed from magma, molten matter from the Earth's core.

20
00:02:00,841 --> 00:02:11,840
Over 500 million years ago, magma, or igneous rock, sluggishly oozed its way toward the surface under tremendous pressure, eventually cooling.

21
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:20,840
Geologists classify rock in three categories, igneous rock, metamorphic, and sedimentary rock.

22
00:02:20,841 --> 00:02:26,796
rock. Igneous rock is often termed the parent rock of the other two forms of rock.

23
00:02:26,797 --> 00:02:27,740
A metamorphic

24
00:02:27,741 --> 00:02:33,120
rock is when you take an igneous rock and you subject that rock to additional heat and

25
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:33,821
pressure.

26
00:02:33,822 --> 00:02:39,340
The stress of the constantly shifting crustal plates forces the chemistry of the

27
00:02:39,341 --> 00:02:40,754
rock to change.

28
00:02:40,755 --> 00:02:47,540
Sedimentary rock is formed from the breakdown of an igneous rock through

29
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:52,660
weathering into its individual pieces.

30
00:02:52,661 --> 00:02:58,620
Because cooled magma in the form of stone, sand, gravel, or sediment covers the Earth's

31
00:02:58,621 --> 00:03:05,000
surface, essentially the planet is one big rock.

32
00:03:05,001 --> 00:03:09,820
And though rock may at first appear worthless because it is so plentiful, virtually everything

33
00:03:09,821 --> 00:03:13,820
we see in modern society contains stone from quarries.

34
00:03:13,821 --> 00:03:19,220
I think it's incomprehensible to even think about modern civilization without stone.

35
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,960
Today, quarrying stone is an immense industry, and quarries of every type are located worldwide.

36
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:34,320
The aggregate business, which includes crust stone and sand and gravel, and the dimension stone business,

37
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:41,940
is the number one non-fuel mineral resource in the world in terms of value and in terms of volume.

38
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:48,220
Mines and quarries both take rocks from an open pit or underground cavern,

39
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:50,100
but their yields are quite different.

40
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:58,060
What constitutes a quarry is the product and not the nature of the opening in the ground.

41
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:02,480
Quarrying would apply to taking out rock,

42
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:07,540
whereas if it's some other mineral or a fossil fuel, we'd call it a mine.

43
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:12,620
The quarrying of stone falls under two major categories,

44
00:04:12,621 --> 00:04:17,977
dimension stone and aggregate.

45
00:04:17,978 --> 00:04:29,940
Granite, marble, limestone, sandstone, and slate are all considered

46
00:04:29,941 --> 00:04:35,260
dimension stone because a quarry's client orders blocks to be sheared from walls in specific

47
00:04:35,261 --> 00:04:37,865
dimensions, usually very large.

48
00:04:37,866 --> 00:04:42,740
Each type of stone is carefully selected for its strength,

49
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,089
durability, quality, and color.

50
00:04:45,090 --> 00:04:48,660
These huge slices are used to create walls and floors

51
00:04:48,661 --> 00:04:55,583
of buildings, headstones, and most of the world's famous monuments.

52
00:04:55,584 --> 00:04:57,340
Sand, gravel, and

53
00:04:57,341 --> 00:05:01,480
crushed stone are called aggregate and are used primarily as ingredients for building

54
00:05:01,481 --> 00:05:06,230
materials, road beds, and an array of modern products.

55
00:05:06,231 --> 00:05:09,220
Granite is one of the most heavily

56
00:05:09,221 --> 00:05:15,442
quarried dimension stones in the world. It is inexpensive and very tough.

57
00:05:15,443 --> 00:05:16,720
Because granite

58
00:05:16,721 --> 00:05:21,720
is so hard to penetrate, it takes massive, powerful drills to split the quarry walls.

59
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:28,422
The method for separating the granite into blocks is called channeling.

60
00:05:28,423 --> 00:05:29,720
Since the 1950s,

61
00:05:29,721 --> 00:05:34,880
In the 1950s, hand-operated jet torch drills have been used to break off the rock.

62
00:05:34,881 --> 00:05:38,880
The torch burns fuel oil at 4,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

63
00:05:38,881 --> 00:05:46,660
But the granite, comprised of several mineral compounds, is neither burned nor melted.

64
00:05:46,661 --> 00:05:51,440
Due to the vast difference in temperatures between flame and stone, molecules in the

65
00:05:51,441 --> 00:05:57,320
various minerals expand at different rates, and the rock flakes away.

66
00:05:57,321 --> 00:06:03,760
The fuel for one jet torch costs $66 an hour to run.

67
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:09,720
So this process is being phased out in favor of pneumatic slot drills.

68
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:16,060
I'm using a slot drill, and what it does is it cuts a line of holes,

69
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:21,500
and then as you cut the line of holes, you take a bigger bit, and then you cut the web drill.

70
00:06:21,501 --> 00:06:28,500
The web is the space drilled out between the holes to divide the block from the quarry wall.

71
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:34,500
This process is called coring the web.

72
00:06:34,501 --> 00:06:38,500
But this method leaves a corrugated surface.

73
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:41,500
More often, a diamond saw is used instead.

74
00:06:41,501 --> 00:06:44,500
It leaves a shear surface.

75
00:06:44,501 --> 00:06:51,380
Because the diamond wire saw cuts such a very thin line, less stone is wasted in the process.

76
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:57,200
To use this technique, pneumatic drills must first be employed to free the back wall

77
00:06:57,201 --> 00:06:59,960
and to create a track for the belt to follow.

78
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:04,740
We must drill down vertically and horizontally to form a shaft.

79
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:10,720
And then diamond impregnated wire is run through that shaft and clamped together

80
00:07:10,721 --> 00:07:13,580
and clamped around a pulley system that's driven by a motor.

81
00:07:13,581 --> 00:07:18,000
and that motor is mounted on a track so that the engine can be moved

82
00:07:18,001 --> 00:07:20,680
to adjust the tension on the wire as the cut is made.

83
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:24,720
To slice the bottom of the block to be removed,

84
00:07:25,180 --> 00:07:27,900
another series of holes is drilled along the bottom plane.

85
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:32,080
Explosives are used to finally dislodge the block.

86
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:35,840
Every third or fourth hole is filled with primer cord explosive

87
00:07:35,841 --> 00:07:37,580
and set off electrically.

88
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:47,680
Another granite block is now free on all sides.

89
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:55,040
But the derricks cannot yet hoist such massive blocks with an average weight of 1,700 tons.

90
00:07:56,140 --> 00:07:59,600
Even for such a high-powered machine, the granite is too heavy.

91
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:04,860
A second drill must be employed to split the block into smaller pieces.

92
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:07,940
We operate a double drill.

93
00:08:07,941 --> 00:08:15,840
Basically, it's two drills hooked up together, and it's sort of like two jackhammers on a two-rail.

94
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:22,360
And then the double drills drill straight down and cut up the chunks like postage stands.

95
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:27,800
Sometimes if a natural crack is observed in the granite, the quarry workers forego the large drills.

96
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:32,280
They use a process known as plugging and feathering, originally invented by the Romans.

97
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:36,560
Granite is much like wood, and it has a grain.

98
00:08:36,561 --> 00:08:41,840
You can make fairly shallow holes and then drive in wedges,

99
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,260
and it will cleave fairly cleanly along the rest.

100
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:48,660
The average weight of a smaller quarried block is 100 tons.

101
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:54,500
Workers string them with thick steel cable, almost 2 inches in diameter.

102
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:57,740
They're hoisted by derrick situated above the quarry.

103
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:03,160
Hand signals from below are received by the derrick men at the cliff's edge

104
00:09:03,161 --> 00:09:07,180
and relayed to the derrick operator, who cannot see the quarry floor.

105
00:09:12,260 --> 00:09:15,720
Once the blocks are pulled out of the quarry, they get set on the banks up there,

106
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:17,820
and they're graded before they come out.

107
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:22,940
Some blocks are shipped out by rail for use in commercial and residential buildings.

108
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:28,340
25% of the granite, though, remains at Rock of Ages,

109
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:31,200
where it is moved to a manufacturing facility.

110
00:09:31,201 --> 00:09:38,000
The granite will be sculpted, sanded and polished into headstones.

111
00:09:38,001 --> 00:09:41,780
The company is one of the leading manufacturers of monuments and headstones in the United

112
00:09:41,781 --> 00:09:42,780
States.

113
00:09:42,781 --> 00:09:48,320
Behind me is a diamond, a small diamond saw that we use to saw up small slabs.

114
00:09:48,321 --> 00:09:53,080
And in this case, the blade is tipped with diamonds.

115
00:09:53,081 --> 00:09:56,680
The diamonds do wear out, but they're harder than the granite.

116
00:09:56,681 --> 00:10:00,640
In that process, the slabs are then taken to our polishing lines.

117
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:05,300
Once we get the product finished, they move into our sandblast area.

118
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:08,360
It's all done with high-pressure hydraulic hoses.

119
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:12,500
Even the hand carvings are now done by little diamond wheels.

120
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:18,440
But technological items such as machine bases are also created at Rock of Ages.

121
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:24,300
A machine base is a flat surface with attachments that machinery can be put on

122
00:10:24,301 --> 00:10:30,340
so that it can remain very stable and it's not affected by changes in temperature and humidity.

123
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:38,320
NASA recently ordered an 18 by 14 foot granite base, the largest known precision base in existence.

124
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:45,260
It will be used as a stable platform on which to test machine parts for NASA's rocket motor propulsion system.

125
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:51,280
This system will power the space shuttle and the next generation of space travel.

126
00:10:51,281 --> 00:10:57,780
Quarry workers today enjoy the most advanced technical equipment available.

127
00:10:58,780 --> 00:11:05,940
But some of the techniques employed are very similar to, if much less hazardous, than those used thousands of years ago.

128
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:14,000
Up next, the dangers of quarrying before modern technology.

129
00:11:14,001 --> 00:11:20,200
A machine for polishing granite introduced in the 1880s was nicknamed the Jenny Lind

130
00:11:20,201 --> 00:11:24,380
after the famous Swedish soprano singer because of its pleasing humming sound.

131
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:27,260
Quarries will return on Modern Marvels.

132
00:11:28,780 --> 00:11:32,360
Despite the sophistication of today's quarrying tools and techniques,

133
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:37,840
the challenge of wresting stone from the earth remains much the same as it was thousands of years ago.

134
00:11:37,841 --> 00:11:46,800
Stone, of course, was extremely valuable to Stone Age man, who used it for weapons and tools.

135
00:11:48,220 --> 00:11:50,477
The first quarries were very simple.

136
00:11:50,478 --> 00:11:53,800
The rock was usually limestone, a rock easy to break.

137
00:11:55,080 --> 00:12:03,020
It was weathered so that the rock was not as firmly in place as a solid wall would be today.

138
00:12:03,021 --> 00:12:14,700
And so they could get at that in the early days, probably using things like rock or crude forms of metal implements that could be used to pry it loose and to break it.

139
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:18,820
Fire was another important way of quarrying rock.

140
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:23,700
A fire would be started atop a limestone outcropping.

141
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:31,280
This created such a vast difference in temperatures within the rock, it would cause a stress fracture, a parting plane to occur.

142
00:12:31,281 --> 00:12:37,420
Heat up the rock, get the rock very hot, scrape the fire off, throw water on,

143
00:12:37,580 --> 00:12:40,320
and then you prise off as much as you can and then you reset the fire.

144
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:45,540
Fire setting was to persist as a method of mining hard rock up into the 19th century.

145
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:47,640
We're still using it in the 21st century.

146
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:51,840
As tougher and more complex tools developed,

147
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:55,660
ancient societies were able to quarry vast amounts of limestone

148
00:12:55,661 --> 00:12:58,440
to build spectacular monuments and dwellings.

149
00:12:59,120 --> 00:12:59,960
The most famous?

150
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:06,340
The Pyramids of Giza, built by the Egyptians over 4,500 years ago to honor their dead.

151
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:10,440
It was the Egyptians who first discovered how to saw rock.

152
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:17,300
The earliest one in existence that we know of was a copper saw that was used with abrasives to cut rock.

153
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:21,640
The Egyptians apparently put carborundum and emery in their saws,

154
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:25,020
and by running it backwards and forwards, you could saw through rock.

155
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:28,240
Today, we use the same technique.

156
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:30,700
but with diamonds instead of sand.

157
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:35,560
The Egyptians also knew how to drill into rock.

158
00:13:35,980 --> 00:13:39,040
They would simply take what would look today like a modern chisel

159
00:13:39,041 --> 00:13:43,560
and basically hit it into the rock and then turn it slightly and hit it again.

160
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:46,140
It would have been a very slow, tedious process.

161
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:48,580
Even more than the Egyptians,

162
00:13:49,100 --> 00:13:52,640
the Romans became masters at quarrying stone to build great cities.

163
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:56,580
Around the first century AD, the height of the Roman Empire,

164
00:13:57,300 --> 00:13:59,560
Municipalities sprang up throughout Italy and Europe.

165
00:14:00,580 --> 00:14:03,020
They have outlasted the empire by many centuries.

166
00:14:03,021 --> 00:14:06,460
If you look at places like Rome and Athens,

167
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:09,640
we still have these wonderful temples.

168
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:13,940
It's incredible how well that rock has stood the test of time.

169
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:16,900
And we see these giant columns that are left.

170
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:20,940
The roads that were built, and we're talking thousands of years,

171
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:24,660
and the buildings are virtually as good today as they were then.

172
00:14:24,661 --> 00:14:33,220
Around 50 AD, the Roman author Pliny the Elder described the technique used to cut stone from its sheer walls.

173
00:14:33,221 --> 00:14:41,520
He described taking metal plates, iron plates, on end like this, moving them back and forth across the rock.

174
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:47,960
And under these plates you would put sand to act as an abrasive because sand is harder than marble.

175
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:52,360
And you would put water to act as a lubricant so that it wouldn't get very hot.

176
00:14:52,361 --> 00:14:59,360
And that same technique that was described by Pliny was used nearly 2,000 years later.

177
00:14:59,361 --> 00:15:06,360
After the Roman Empire fell, Europe no longer had a sophisticated network of trade or industry.

178
00:15:06,361 --> 00:15:16,360
Architects wishing to construct stone buildings usually resorted to robbing pre-cut quarried stone from deteriorating Roman-era buildings.

179
00:15:16,361 --> 00:15:24,860
quarrying resumed in the middle ages this time to build castles towns and fortresses

180
00:15:24,861 --> 00:15:33,700
but the yield was small and the work laborious black powder from china used for centuries in

181
00:15:33,701 --> 00:15:38,080
europe for fireworks was put to use as an explosive in the 17th century

182
00:15:38,081 --> 00:15:44,020
they're very ineffective explosive but it was the only thing that they had and it was certainly

183
00:15:44,021 --> 00:15:47,900
better than having to get a fire setting.

184
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:53,320
With the Industrial Revolution came vast technological improvements.

185
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:56,380
Harder steel meant stronger tools.

186
00:15:57,080 --> 00:16:00,140
But the labor required to rest the stone was strenuous.

187
00:16:01,140 --> 00:16:03,480
They had what we called a T-drill.

188
00:16:03,481 --> 00:16:07,120
A gentleman in the middle would hold on to the drill and rotate it,

189
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:08,900
and rotate the point against the stone.

190
00:16:09,380 --> 00:16:12,660
A man on each side would alternately hit it with a sledge,

191
00:16:12,661 --> 00:16:16,180
and little by little they would grind a hole into the stone.

192
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:22,100
Transporting huge blocks of stone was equally difficult.

193
00:16:22,740 --> 00:16:27,440
The strongest team of horses could barely pull the quarried blocks over mountains.

194
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:32,060
Getting them down a steep incline was even worse.

195
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:37,220
They would have horse teams hitched in the rear as well as in the front

196
00:16:37,221 --> 00:16:39,280
so that they could pull backwards on the wagon.

197
00:16:39,281 --> 00:16:45,560
Sometimes a wagon would break away and go to the bottom of the hill, and someone would be killed in the process.

198
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:49,500
For the largest blocks, it was even slower going.

199
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:51,907
They'd have a series of rollers.

200
00:16:51,908 --> 00:16:54,840
It'd be like large logs or dowels underneath the block,

201
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,140
and you'd have to keep putting them in front of the block and rolling it down.

202
00:16:58,620 --> 00:17:02,340
It might take about a day to travel a mile.

203
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:07,660
Early quarry entrepreneurs would have to wait for a railway system

204
00:17:07,661 --> 00:17:10,420
that could transport quarried blocks to distant areas.

205
00:17:11,440 --> 00:17:13,740
As railroads stretched across the states,

206
00:17:14,340 --> 00:17:17,920
quarrymen from all over Europe hastened to work in American quarries.

207
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:21,980
Some of the most highly skilled and sought-after laborers

208
00:17:21,981 --> 00:17:23,240
were those from Italy,

209
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:26,040
who would carve granite and other dimension stone

210
00:17:26,041 --> 00:17:29,880
into intricate designs for headstones and monuments.

211
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:34,020
But for workers in the quarries,

212
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:37,280
the work was grueling and life-threatening.

213
00:17:37,660 --> 00:17:42,080
You could find equipment falling off of ledges and landing on people.

214
00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:48,280
They would carry long steel rods on their shoulders going down long, skinny ladders.

215
00:17:48,281 --> 00:17:52,400
And sometimes the rungs would break on the ladders or the side rails would break

216
00:17:52,401 --> 00:17:56,380
and the people would have to drop their equipment to grab onto the ladder.

217
00:17:57,220 --> 00:18:02,200
Many workers were crushed by stones as they moved out of place.

218
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:05,100
Some were killed by black powder.

219
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:10,820
A spark would set it off and an explosion would occur and workers would be killed.

220
00:18:10,980 --> 00:18:12,860
People also had problems with their eyes.

221
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:17,960
They would hit steel rods into rock and pieces of steel would fall off

222
00:18:17,961 --> 00:18:22,180
or pieces of rock would splinter off and would blind people.

223
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:29,800
Until the end of the 19th century, oxen, mules and horses were the workers' only form of automation.

224
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:32,460
There were no gas engines, there were no steam engines.

225
00:18:32,461 --> 00:18:35,840
They'd drop a line down from that derrick and hook it to the rocks,

226
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,660
and then they'd have an oxen going around the raceway,

227
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:43,540
which would take up that line and would pull the rock up out of the quarry.

228
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:46,720
It would do it maybe a foot a minute, a very slow job.

229
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:50,800
The mules actually lived in the quarry, and that was really their world,

230
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:53,840
moving carts of stone over to the edge of the quarry.

231
00:18:54,820 --> 00:18:57,100
As steam-powered engines became available,

232
00:18:57,760 --> 00:19:01,460
new derricks were installed, replacing the animal-drawn pulley systems.

233
00:19:02,460 --> 00:19:10,700
The use of steam drills employed from about 1870 to 1920 allowed granite to be quarried at a much faster rate.

234
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:16,880
Since the 1890s, electric batteries have provided a far safer method of detonating explosives.

235
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:20,680
Before that, the men worked dangerously close to the dynamite.

236
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:25,180
Air-driven pneumatic drills were widely used by the 1920s.

237
00:19:25,181 --> 00:19:30,420
They were a boom to the industry, but the men operating them paid dearly because of the dust.

238
00:19:30,421 --> 00:19:34,520
workers began developing a lung disease known as silicosis

239
00:19:34,521 --> 00:19:39,360
at first many people blamed the lung disease on tuberculosis

240
00:19:39,361 --> 00:19:42,240
which was a common killer in the united states at that time

241
00:19:42,241 --> 00:19:45,800
but an inordinate amount of quarry men kept dying

242
00:19:45,801 --> 00:19:50,360
the life span of a miner at that point would have been somewhere between

243
00:19:50,361 --> 00:19:52,140
six and twenty four months

244
00:19:52,141 --> 00:19:54,800
the drills became known as widow makers

245
00:19:54,801 --> 00:20:01,640
After discovering the connection between the quarrymen's diseased lungs and the quarry dust,

246
00:20:02,180 --> 00:20:05,000
a drill was invented in 1930 to solve the problem.

247
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:11,680
A steady stream of water was added that ran down the drill shaft to catch the dust before it could rise,

248
00:20:12,340 --> 00:20:14,400
saving countless quarry workers' lives.

249
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:21,540
In the middle of the 20th century, more efficient explosives came into use,

250
00:20:21,541 --> 00:20:25,900
and diamond saws led to an incredible expansion of the quarrying industry.

251
00:20:27,120 --> 00:20:31,380
Today, quarry workers enjoy a high level of protection from on-the-job accidents.

252
00:20:32,220 --> 00:20:36,880
But sometimes the structures that come from quarries need to be protected too.

253
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:42,340
Up next, one of America's most famous landmarks gets a facelift

254
00:20:42,341 --> 00:20:46,940
with a beautiful and highly prized stone, marble.

255
00:20:46,941 --> 00:20:53,940
When the Cemetery of the Innocents in Paris was filled beyond capacity in the 18th century,

256
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:59,520
thousands of skeletons were dug up and placed in abandoned underground Parisian quarries,

257
00:20:59,800 --> 00:21:00,980
where they remain today.

258
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:05,340
Quarries will return on Modern Marvels.

259
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:10,100
The Greeks first quarried it.

260
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:13,360
The Romans took it to new heights.

261
00:21:13,361 --> 00:21:17,840
and some of America's most famous buildings, like the Capitol,

262
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:20,340
the New York Stock Exchange,

263
00:21:20,900 --> 00:21:25,140
and the Lincoln Memorial are made from it.

264
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:30,840
The most intricate columns, facades, and cornices are sculpted from it.

265
00:21:31,460 --> 00:21:35,180
This is the most highly prized rock in the world, marble.

266
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:39,420
The allure of marble lies in its beautifully textured colors,

267
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:41,580
its sparkle, and its softness.

268
00:21:42,540 --> 00:21:45,380
Artisans are easily able to sculpt it by hand.

269
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,680
It's people working with the rock to create things.

270
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:51,560
They don't tear it apart.

271
00:21:51,561 --> 00:21:55,780
They go out and they carefully try to remove large pieces of rock,

272
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:58,740
and then that rock will be taken and cut into smaller pieces

273
00:21:58,741 --> 00:22:03,800
and carefully polished or handled, cut by human beings.

274
00:22:04,200 --> 00:22:06,840
People working with rocks to create pieces of art.

275
00:22:07,460 --> 00:22:10,140
And that's kind of what makes it the romantic side of the industry.

276
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:14,680
Marble curiously was formed from a living organism.

277
00:22:17,040 --> 00:22:22,700
Tiny crustaceans in ancient oceans absorbed calcium carbonate sediment, a form of rock.

278
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:29,180
Over millions of years, their shells accumulated on the ocean floor.

279
00:22:31,320 --> 00:22:37,620
Heat, pressure and eons of upheaval created the deposits of marble we quarry today.

280
00:22:41,580 --> 00:22:48,220
Although Italy is known for its beautiful marble, there are American deposits that rival the beauty of the Italian stone.

281
00:22:51,560 --> 00:23:00,360
This one marble deposit, directly under the Georgia Marble Company in Tate, Georgia, contains over 30 varieties of marble and limestone.

282
00:23:01,720 --> 00:23:06,700
In fact, the Georgia Marble Company is situated over one of the world's largest veins of marble.

283
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:12,480
Even down in the quarry, with only the raw marble exposed, its beauty is apparent.

284
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:18,640
Because marble is more fragile than granite, different techniques are used to quarry it.

285
00:23:19,580 --> 00:23:27,300
Cutting the stone here, the mineralogy, doesn't support using a torch like you probably saw in some of the granite operations.

286
00:23:27,301 --> 00:23:35,040
The stone here is a softer material, lends itself to being cut with diamonds rather than a torch.

287
00:23:35,960 --> 00:23:39,160
Diamond drills are the first choice for both granite and marble.

288
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:45,180
Though marble is a much softer stone, the drill is used for the same reason granite couriers use it.

289
00:23:45,580 --> 00:23:47,380
It leaves a sheer surface on the block.

290
00:23:47,381 --> 00:23:53,460
The saws we're using here are thin belt saws where the diamond segments are held in by a rubber compound.

291
00:23:54,320 --> 00:23:57,300
And it's just like a blade on a belt.

292
00:23:58,700 --> 00:24:01,420
The diamonds are inexpensive, tiny chips.

293
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:04,960
The belts measure almost 12 feet in length.

294
00:24:05,040 --> 00:24:11,380
This saw here cuts vertically.

295
00:24:11,381 --> 00:24:16,760
It cuts the strips and then I come along and cut them up in sections.

296
00:24:16,761 --> 00:24:20,460
It cuts, basically the way it's doing now, it cuts two inches a minute.

297
00:24:20,461 --> 00:24:25,040
A good belt can average anywhere from 60 to 80 foot a day.

298
00:24:25,041 --> 00:24:32,860
When we're cutting the stone we'll come in with our horizontal belt saws and do the bottom

299
00:24:32,861 --> 00:24:36,960
cut on the block to take the block loose from the core.

300
00:24:36,961 --> 00:24:41,960
The stone is basically cut in a grid pattern, much like a checkerboard.

301
00:24:41,961 --> 00:24:48,520
To break the block into smaller, more manageable pieces, the Roman technique of plugging and

302
00:24:48,521 --> 00:24:50,020
feathering is still employed.

303
00:24:50,021 --> 00:24:56,860
To split a block, we drill a series of holes, and then we take steel wedges, sometimes wood,

304
00:24:56,861 --> 00:25:00,860
drive them in with a sledgehammer from hole to hole.

305
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:06,860
And that will create the stone to break between holes, break it loose.

306
00:25:11,860 --> 00:25:14,860
Technology has not changed in that part of it at this point.

307
00:25:15,860 --> 00:25:21,860
500 horsepower front loaders with 7-foot tires haul the 20-ton blocks out of the quarries.

308
00:25:26,860 --> 00:25:35,240
The quarried marble is taken to a manufacturing house to be cut and polished to fit exactly into or onto a building or monument.

309
00:25:35,900 --> 00:25:43,180
The Lincoln Memorial comprises of 28 different pieces of marble that was all hand carved and fitted together.

310
00:25:43,420 --> 00:25:45,320
One of those pieces weighs over 40 ton.

311
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:48,880
The Lincoln Memorial stands almost 20 feet.

312
00:25:48,881 --> 00:25:57,580
So a lot of hand carving, a lot of pieces that had to be precisely cut, precisely carved, that they all fit together so it looks like one seamless piece of marble.

313
00:25:58,600 --> 00:26:04,380
One of Georgia marble's biggest challenges was to refurbish the Capitol building in Washington, D.C.

314
00:26:05,280 --> 00:26:09,840
In 1958, the government realized the building sorely needed a facelift.

315
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:12,760
But not because marble had deteriorated.

316
00:26:13,260 --> 00:26:18,180
The Capitol had originally been made of quarried sandstone, a much less durable rock.

317
00:26:18,181 --> 00:26:25,480
They had original blueprints, but a lot of it was taken off of the building and cast made out of plaster of Paris.

318
00:26:25,980 --> 00:26:30,480
And those pieces were shipped here and then duplicated and re-carved, identical.

319
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:37,080
Some imagination was used because it was so deteriorated that they couldn't tell what the original design may have been on some of the corners.

320
00:26:37,320 --> 00:26:39,040
So they did their best to match it.

321
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:44,380
The east wing of the capital required some very large pieces, especially on the columns.

322
00:26:44,381 --> 00:26:49,100
There was more than 20 columns used that was, I think, between 20 and 30 feet long.

323
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:50,880
So they would go down into the quarry,

324
00:26:51,460 --> 00:26:55,140
hand-picked sights that the structure and integrity of the stone looked good,

325
00:26:55,440 --> 00:26:58,240
so they knew they could get a 20- or 30-foot piece out of it.

326
00:26:58,520 --> 00:27:01,800
And then it was put on various turning lathes and just chipped away.

327
00:27:03,720 --> 00:27:07,400
Once shipped to the site, the pieces were laid out in front of the Capitol.

328
00:27:07,401 --> 00:27:17,220
There was a lot of people, like hundreds of people out there on scaffolds,

329
00:27:17,580 --> 00:27:20,440
actually going piece by piece, which they were marked,

330
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:24,280
picking them up with derricks and picking them up and putting them in place.

331
00:27:24,860 --> 00:27:27,780
The Capitol's refurbishing took two years to finish.

332
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:32,840
Work was completed just in time for President Kennedy's inaugural address in 1960.

333
00:27:32,841 --> 00:27:37,140
I will faithfully execute the office of President of the United States.

334
00:27:37,400 --> 00:27:42,400
No building material yet manufactured rivals the natural beauty of marble.

335
00:27:43,900 --> 00:27:48,300
Today the marble industry thrives, and not just because of dimension stone.

336
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:55,140
Marble, along with granite and many other types of rock, are crushed and used for an astonishing range of products.

337
00:27:57,260 --> 00:28:00,200
Up next, quarrying goes underground.

338
00:28:03,420 --> 00:28:05,520
Marble is a form of limestone.

339
00:28:05,521 --> 00:28:12,520
Limestone can be processed to create calcium oxide, and when the powder is heated, it becomes incandescent.

340
00:28:12,521 --> 00:28:17,045
In the 19th century, it was used in theaters to light the stage.

341
00:28:17,046 --> 00:28:19,520
Hence the saying, in the limelight.

342
00:28:19,521 --> 00:28:22,520
Quarries will return on Modern Marvelous.

343
00:28:22,521 --> 00:28:26,520
Imagine your house without modern building materials.

344
00:28:26,521 --> 00:28:33,520
Take away the quarried ingredients in concrete, paint, and glass, and you're left with, well, nothing.

345
00:28:33,521 --> 00:28:39,420
That's because crushed rock, sand and gravel, collectively called aggregate, is in virtually

346
00:28:39,421 --> 00:28:46,320
every building material used today.

347
00:28:46,321 --> 00:28:51,300
Society as we know it would not exist, because the absence of aggregate would mean no highways,

348
00:28:51,301 --> 00:28:58,300
railroad beds, airports, no infrastructure.

349
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:09,400
This is the Luckstone Quarry in Fairfax, Virginia,

350
00:29:09,401 --> 00:29:15,680
and it's one of the largest aggregate quarriers in the United States.

351
00:29:15,681 --> 00:29:18,620
There are no saws in this quarry.

352
00:29:18,621 --> 00:29:21,180
Explosives are aggregate quarrying.

353
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:26,520
Limestone, dolomite, and granite are the most common rocks quarried for aggregate production.

354
00:29:27,460 --> 00:29:31,980
A truck with a hydraulic hole digger attached to its bed creates the holes.

355
00:29:33,580 --> 00:29:38,820
Then, a truck carrying explosive material follows the men who place the detonator caps.

356
00:29:39,400 --> 00:29:44,920
The detonator ignites the booster when it's inserted properly.

357
00:29:44,921 --> 00:29:50,920
This is what ignites the ammonium nitrate that comes off of the bulk truck.

358
00:29:50,921 --> 00:29:53,920
The explosives are designed to move a certain amount of rock,

359
00:29:53,921 --> 00:29:59,920
and we know exactly what that pattern should be here in the different areas of the quarry.

360
00:29:59,921 --> 00:30:06,920
So this particular shot is 15 feet of rock in front of the hole and 13 feet between the holes,

361
00:30:06,921 --> 00:30:12,920
and then we can measure the different heights of the wall to determine how much

362
00:30:12,921 --> 00:30:18,460
much and what type of explosive we want to put in the hole.

363
00:30:18,461 --> 00:30:23,640
Each incendiary device is powerful enough to explode 20 tons of rock.

364
00:30:23,641 --> 00:30:37,641
And when combined, these explosives carve out over a thousand tons of rock and soil.

365
00:30:38,360 --> 00:30:42,480
After the blast, a water truck sprays the area to keep dust at a minimum.

366
00:30:44,020 --> 00:30:50,520
Bulldozers drive into the explosion area and begin scooping the crushed rock into trucks with huge payloads.

367
00:30:51,520 --> 00:30:55,700
The trucks then head for the load tower, where the crushing process begins.

368
00:31:00,060 --> 00:31:04,420
The primary crusher is made up of a hopper, a feeder, and our gyratory crusher.

369
00:31:04,421 --> 00:31:08,920
The trucks back up to the hopper, dump the load into the hopper.

370
00:31:08,921 --> 00:31:12,720
A feeder then is turned on at a controlled rate, feeds the crusher.

371
00:31:12,721 --> 00:31:20,720
The crusher crushes the rock from approximately 30 inches down to about 10 inches.

372
00:31:20,721 --> 00:31:25,420
This material falls onto a series of belts where it feeds into our surge pile.

373
00:31:25,421 --> 00:31:28,720
At that point, the secondary operator will take over.

374
00:31:28,721 --> 00:31:31,920
His job is to empty the primary surge pile.

375
00:31:31,921 --> 00:31:35,220
He does this by a group of feeders and conveyors.

376
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:41,740
Following the secondary, the tertiary plant begins.

377
00:31:42,180 --> 00:31:45,080
This is the final stage of our crushing operation,

378
00:31:45,220 --> 00:31:47,280
where our material is conveyed to our screens.

379
00:31:47,880 --> 00:31:51,460
The screens function is to separate the material into different size fractions

380
00:31:51,461 --> 00:31:55,600
to meet the specific needs of our customer's specifications.

381
00:31:57,180 --> 00:32:01,340
Each hour, 1,500 tons of rock course through the structure.

382
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:09,920
In the early days of quarrying, plant operations were made up of large banks of start-stop switches.

383
00:32:09,921 --> 00:32:12,920
It was a large control panel that controlled all the conveyors.

384
00:32:12,921 --> 00:32:18,920
The least bit of a change in a vibration could signify a problem in the plant,

385
00:32:18,921 --> 00:32:21,920
and a good operator would know which conveyors to turn off.

386
00:32:21,921 --> 00:32:25,920
With automation, such as we have here at Lugstone, this has been eliminated.

387
00:32:25,921 --> 00:32:29,920
The computer itself runs the plant and it monitors all the systems,

388
00:32:29,921 --> 00:32:36,920
systems and this optimizes the production of stone in our operations.

389
00:32:37,240 --> 00:32:42,100
New construction and building techniques have created a greater demand for aggregate.

390
00:32:42,101 --> 00:32:48,200
And greater demand for this product has produced more creative ways to quarry.

391
00:32:48,201 --> 00:32:52,180
Some operations dredge rivers and the ocean for aggregate.

392
00:32:52,181 --> 00:32:55,920
And some quarries take their operations underground.

393
00:32:55,921 --> 00:33:00,380
find rocks with big huge folds in them or rocks that plunge down into the earth.

394
00:33:00,860 --> 00:33:04,940
And so you start out with a surface quarry and next thing you know you're in an underground

395
00:33:04,941 --> 00:33:07,880
mine as you're chasing this rock down into the earth.

396
00:33:08,500 --> 00:33:11,100
This underground quarry has done just that.

397
00:33:11,620 --> 00:33:13,640
And once again, with explosives.

398
00:33:14,680 --> 00:33:19,740
What we're doing right here, we are loading and fixing the blasts tonight.

399
00:33:19,741 --> 00:33:23,120
It's called Unimax, but it's just a stick of dynamite.

400
00:33:23,200 --> 00:33:24,440
It's about 8% dynamite.

401
00:33:24,441 --> 00:33:28,100
And this is what we call a non-L blasting cap.

402
00:33:28,540 --> 00:33:32,660
What we do, you stick the cap into this dynamite right here,

403
00:33:33,380 --> 00:33:35,120
and then you do a little twist.

404
00:33:36,120 --> 00:33:40,500
And we take this right here, and we stick that in the hole,

405
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:42,880
and then we blow ammonia nitrate in the hole.

406
00:33:43,460 --> 00:33:47,500
And then after that, we'll put 220 volts down here,

407
00:33:47,960 --> 00:33:50,120
and what it does, it'll trigger that electrical cap,

408
00:33:50,240 --> 00:33:53,080
and then in turn, it'll set this dead cord off.

409
00:33:53,081 --> 00:33:58,440
And when they splashed, they should be about, probably about 1,100 ton of stone laying here.

410
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:05,240
People and cameras are not allowed underground during the explosion because it is too dangerous.

411
00:34:07,260 --> 00:34:14,240
After the dust settles, a quarry worker drives a 35-ton truck called a claw over to the rock pile

412
00:34:14,241 --> 00:34:17,500
and scrapes the ceiling to ensure against rock slides.

413
00:34:18,640 --> 00:34:22,460
The aggregate is then driven out of the mine to the load tower for processing.

414
00:34:23,080 --> 00:34:31,891
Unlike the dimension stone industry, the aggregate industry is relatively new.

415
00:34:31,892 --> 00:34:33,360
It started in

416
00:34:33,361 --> 00:34:39,060
the late 19th century due to the demand for road paving materials and the invention of

417
00:34:39,061 --> 00:34:46,260
a formula that would change the look of modern society forever, Portland cement.

418
00:34:46,261 --> 00:34:47,160
Cement was

419
00:34:47,161 --> 00:34:52,308
used to create concrete, the building block of the modern world.

420
00:34:52,309 --> 00:34:54,480
Why a demand for aggregate?

421
00:34:54,481 --> 00:35:01,160
The ingredients for concrete's powerfully durable mixture, sand, gravel and limestone.

422
00:35:01,161 --> 00:35:05,800
And quarrymen once again were asked to step up to the laborious task of wresting rock

423
00:35:05,801 --> 00:35:06,800
from the earth.

424
00:35:06,801 --> 00:35:13,200
In those early days, the extraction of the stone was done by drilling and it took a month

425
00:35:13,201 --> 00:35:14,756
to drill five holes.

426
00:35:14,757 --> 00:35:20,200
Dynamite was put down in those holes, and then the stone was dynamited

427
00:35:20,540 --> 00:35:27,540
out. It came out in big, large chunks. It was not very efficient.

428
00:35:27,541 --> 00:35:33,480
Many quarrymen were reluctant to line up for the backbreaking labor of making aggregate,

429
00:35:33,481 --> 00:35:39,480
because after the rock was exploded, their only tool was a sledgehammer.

430
00:35:39,481 --> 00:35:40,480
Enterprising

431
00:35:40,481 --> 00:35:44,288
quarry owners found a solution. Prison chain gangs.

432
00:35:44,289 --> 00:35:47,200
Tens of thousands of men were sentenced

433
00:35:47,201 --> 00:35:48,994
to years of hard labor.

434
00:35:48,995 --> 00:35:54,920
Then the guys would take a month in order to load all that stone up by hand.

435
00:35:55,360 --> 00:36:01,480
They would oftentimes have to crush it again with dynamite on top of the rock on the big boulders

436
00:36:01,481 --> 00:36:05,558
and get it to a size that they could physically load into a cart by hand.

437
00:36:05,559 --> 00:36:06,620
They would load the

438
00:36:06,621 --> 00:36:07,559
rock onto carts.

439
00:36:07,560 --> 00:36:11,960
They'd break them into what they called man-sized pieces, which meant a man

440
00:36:11,961 --> 00:36:16,720
could lift this rock and heave it into a cart, where it would then be hauled out of the mine

441
00:36:16,721 --> 00:36:18,332
by a donkey or a mule.

442
00:36:18,333 --> 00:36:23,680
The carts would be pulled over to the point in the quarry where there was

443
00:36:23,681 --> 00:36:30,095
a crane, and that crane was attached to the basket and hoisted up out of the quarry.

444
00:36:30,096 --> 00:36:31,240
When the demand

445
00:36:31,241 --> 00:36:36,028
became great, the people couldn't keep up, and so they had to find a way to do things better.

446
00:36:36,029 --> 00:36:36,729
A number

447
00:36:36,730 --> 00:36:39,280
of different types of crushers were invented to crush the rock.

448
00:36:39,780 --> 00:36:44,260
The industry focused on finding new and better blasting techniques

449
00:36:44,261 --> 00:36:46,860
through either changing the patterns of holes

450
00:36:46,861 --> 00:36:48,780
or changing the timing of the blast

451
00:36:48,781 --> 00:36:50,800
so that you have a sequential blast pattern

452
00:36:50,801 --> 00:36:53,820
or changing the type of explosive that you used.

453
00:36:56,580 --> 00:36:59,320
Today, different blasting and crushing techniques

454
00:36:59,321 --> 00:37:02,460
are also used to create specific rock shapes and sizes,

455
00:37:03,020 --> 00:37:05,520
which are required to produce many consumer items.

456
00:37:06,440 --> 00:37:11,200
You might say, well, where's stone and paper?

457
00:37:11,540 --> 00:37:15,440
Well, actually, it's a very fine coating on the surface of the paper.

458
00:37:15,441 --> 00:37:18,780
And that's what makes the ball on your ballpoint pen roll.

459
00:37:19,100 --> 00:37:20,320
But that's just the beginning.

460
00:37:20,940 --> 00:37:22,460
We actually eat rocks.

461
00:37:23,360 --> 00:37:27,840
If you look on the package of Tums, it says the active ingredient is calcium carbonate,

462
00:37:28,140 --> 00:37:29,320
which is what marble is.

463
00:37:30,020 --> 00:37:32,920
Marble is known to have 1,001 uses.

464
00:37:33,180 --> 00:37:35,760
It's used to keep chewing gum from sticking to the wrapper,

465
00:37:35,761 --> 00:37:39,200
the little white powder there is sweetened to marble dust.

466
00:37:40,100 --> 00:37:42,120
Because of this incredible demand,

467
00:37:42,560 --> 00:37:46,580
crushed stone, sand, and gravel quarries are in almost every town and city.

468
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:52,740
But the thousands of aggregate quarries usually go unnoticed

469
00:37:52,741 --> 00:37:55,100
because they are hidden behind trees and hills.

470
00:37:56,660 --> 00:37:59,740
In fact, not only do you rarely see a quarry,

471
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:02,880
your house could be sitting on a reclaimed quarry,

472
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:04,440
and you wouldn't even know it.

473
00:38:04,441 --> 00:38:11,440
Up next, how to convert a former quarry into a work of art.

474
00:38:11,441 --> 00:38:16,440
One mile of a two-lane highway requires over 40,000 tons of aggregate.

475
00:38:16,441 --> 00:38:21,440
There are 3.9 million miles of these roadways in the United States.

476
00:38:21,441 --> 00:38:25,440
Quarries will return on Modern Marvels.

477
00:38:25,441 --> 00:38:30,440
There are over 10,000 quarries in the United States alone.

478
00:38:30,441 --> 00:38:34,420
and the design and construction of each one is painstakingly planned.

479
00:38:35,420 --> 00:38:38,200
After locating a large deposit of suitable rock,

480
00:38:38,940 --> 00:38:41,260
quarriers purchase huge tracts of land,

481
00:38:41,880 --> 00:38:46,200
ranging from a few acres to over three square miles of surface area.

482
00:38:47,580 --> 00:38:51,740
The first thing you would start with would be to quadrill the property

483
00:38:51,741 --> 00:38:57,080
to make sure that the rock down there meets all the specifications that we need to meet.

484
00:38:57,081 --> 00:39:05,120
And assuming we found that we have a good deposit for those purposes, then we need to go to our local county to get permits.

485
00:39:05,420 --> 00:39:12,260
We develop the quarry itself, which we do by, first of all, taking off the overburden, which is simply soil.

486
00:39:12,780 --> 00:39:19,440
While we're developing the quarry, we're also building a processing plant for that material.

487
00:39:19,441 --> 00:39:25,520
and you're looking at something that's probably the beginning cost is a million dollars

488
00:39:25,521 --> 00:39:27,620
and can go anywhere up from there.

489
00:39:28,820 --> 00:39:30,440
Quarries are difficult to detect.

490
00:39:31,220 --> 00:39:36,260
Most of the time they are cleverly disguised from the public by what the industry calls berms.

491
00:39:36,261 --> 00:39:45,000
A berm is basically a long hill formed by topsoil that was first taken off the mine site when you develop it.

492
00:39:45,001 --> 00:39:47,000
Trees are often planted on top of them.

493
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:55,800
And the berms become a way of restricting the visual impact of the mine site from the general public.

494
00:39:56,120 --> 00:39:58,320
It's also used for noise abatement.

495
00:39:59,680 --> 00:40:03,620
Quarries operate until the rock deposit is completely exhausted.

496
00:40:06,580 --> 00:40:13,720
When a quarry is closed, quarriers usually must return the land to its original state or other beneficial use.

497
00:40:14,420 --> 00:40:16,200
This process is called reclamation.

498
00:40:17,800 --> 00:40:21,520
Many quarriers plan for reclaiming the land before they begin quarrying.

499
00:40:22,160 --> 00:40:25,880
The geologists, the mining engineers, the management team

500
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:30,720
has a goal in mind of what they want the quarry to look like at the end.

501
00:40:32,240 --> 00:40:34,900
Reclamation of a quarry is important to communities

502
00:40:34,901 --> 00:40:38,420
because residents don't want scarred landscape near their property.

503
00:40:38,421 --> 00:40:45,700
The land value where the quarry is situated, in most cases, has greatly appreciated over

504
00:40:45,701 --> 00:40:47,900
the life of the quarry.

505
00:40:47,901 --> 00:40:53,800
The companies often find that they can sell or lease lots of land around their mine sites,

506
00:40:53,801 --> 00:40:58,660
often for more money than what they got from the raw material being excavated from the

507
00:40:58,661 --> 00:40:59,660
site.

508
00:40:59,661 --> 00:41:06,020
When a quarry is reclaimed, even the material from its berms is not enough to fill the hole.

509
00:41:06,021 --> 00:41:11,760
These are in the community for a long time, 10, 20, 50, 100 or more years at a time.

510
00:41:11,761 --> 00:41:14,960
You can imagine the amount of material that is removed from a quarry over that amount

511
00:41:14,961 --> 00:41:15,960
of time.

512
00:41:15,961 --> 00:41:20,460
You're not going to have that same material around the mine site to backfill with.

513
00:41:20,461 --> 00:41:26,940
So, usually you have to come up with some sort of innovative reclamation.

514
00:41:26,941 --> 00:41:33,420
The Quarry Golf Club in San Antonio, Texas is an example of contouring with limited backfill.

515
00:41:33,421 --> 00:41:45,660
The area was a former aggregate operation, and the quarry walls are still visible.

516
00:41:45,661 --> 00:41:53,080
Reclaimed quarries have been turned into parks, wildlife habitats, farmland, and recreation

517
00:41:53,081 --> 00:41:56,500
areas such as lakes.

518
00:41:56,501 --> 00:42:02,520
Quarries that are filled with water are often used as recreation areas for scuba diving,

519
00:42:02,521 --> 00:42:06,080
for swimming, for beaches, things of that nature.

520
00:42:06,480 --> 00:42:09,600
Even underground quarries are reused after all the rock is harvested.

521
00:42:10,120 --> 00:42:13,460
The underground people, you know, they use it for storage.

522
00:42:13,920 --> 00:42:16,380
They become offices climate-controlled

523
00:42:16,381 --> 00:42:19,740
because once you get underground, it's 55 degrees year-round.

524
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:24,380
Many creative things have also been done with abandoned quarries

525
00:42:24,381 --> 00:42:25,780
which have not been reclaimed.

526
00:42:27,060 --> 00:42:31,660
One such quarry in Sheffield, England, called Portland Sculptor Trust,

527
00:42:31,661 --> 00:42:35,760
allows artists to come in and sculpt designs from loose stone

528
00:42:35,761 --> 00:42:39,800
or carve three-dimensional designs directly from the sheer quarry walls.

529
00:42:40,520 --> 00:42:43,480
They invite people in for a period of about two weeks.

530
00:42:43,620 --> 00:42:48,360
We'll train you to become a sculptor using stone from that quarry.

531
00:42:48,361 --> 00:42:52,860
And then you can leave your masterpiece there if it's good enough or take it home with you.

532
00:42:54,320 --> 00:42:58,940
Though quarriers use many of the same or similar techniques used by the ancients,

533
00:42:58,941 --> 00:43:02,860
their burgeoning technology holds some fascinating possibilities.

534
00:43:04,600 --> 00:43:07,340
New quarrying techniques will continually be created

535
00:43:07,341 --> 00:43:11,800
to keep pace with the huge demand for dimension stone and aggregate products.

536
00:43:13,600 --> 00:43:19,200
The quarry industry must keep expanding to supply the world's ever-increasing needs.

537
00:43:22,540 --> 00:43:25,760
Highways will be repaved and new ones built.

538
00:43:25,761 --> 00:43:32,760
More buildings and housing will be planned for an expanding population.

539
00:43:32,761 --> 00:43:38,760
And new, currently unimaginable products will be created, using rock.

540
00:43:38,761 --> 00:43:44,760
And since the Earth is comprised of 6.5 billion trillion tons of rock,

541
00:43:44,761 --> 00:43:47,760
its supply may never run out.

542
00:43:47,761 --> 00:43:52,760
We have literally only scratched the surface.

543
00:43:52,761 --> 00:43:56,680
The prospect of quarrying millenniums into the future appears certain.

544
00:43:59,260 --> 00:44:02,920
The new Stone Age, it seems, has just begun.

